<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter III of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1762">1762</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter III was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for six months in 1762, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> raised in Holstein. In 1745 he married Sophie of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, who took the name Catherine. His abrupt peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and unpopular policies led to a coup in which Catherine seized the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>, and he was killed soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia.jpg" alt="Peter III of Russia" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Conrad Pfandzelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronation_portrait_of_Peter_III_of_Russia_-1761.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Charles Frederick's son became Tsar <b>Peter III of Russia</b>, and in 1773 Peter's son Paul exchanged the Gottorp part of Holstein with Denmark for Oldenburg, ending the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Duchy of Holstein-Gottorp</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most famous member was Sophie Auguste Friederike, daughter of Prince Christian August, who married the future <b>Peter III</b> and became Empress Catherine II of Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Ropsha Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.32,10.14&amp;year=1750" aria-label="Kiel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 302 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter III of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 572h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 572h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.32,10.14&amp;year=1750">What was here in 1750? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
